# The cooler's two nodes, tagged so the logged-in user may open them.
#
# hotaru needs no root: systemd-logind puts an ACL on a device tagged
# "uaccess" for whoever is logged in at the seat. Without a rule that applies
# the tag, hotaru finds the cooler and cannot open it -- lighting works,
# telemetry and the screen do not, and the reason is invisible.
#
# On the machine this was written on that rule came from the liquidctl
# package, which hotaru no longer depends on (spec 012). A machine that never
# had liquidctl installed has no such rule, which is exactly the "someone
# else's machine" failure spec 001 exists to prevent.
#
# Two rules because the two interfaces surface differently: the status and
# control channel as a hidraw character device, the screen's bulk endpoint as
# the USB device node itself.
#
# 1e71 is NZXT. The products hotaru drives are in internal/cooler/discover.go;
# the vendor is matched here rather than the product because a rule that
# listed products would have to be edited in step with that table, and a
# uaccess tag on a device hotaru does not drive costs nothing.

KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1e71", TAG+="uaccess"
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1e71", TAG+="uaccess"
